ralph/progress.txt:623:- Per-subtype quota dimension on the services Create unit of work (BE-0326 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go ServiceService.Create + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_applications_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0327 compose_stacks, BE-0328 databases — every per-subtype dimension whose denominator is a SUBSET of the services table filtered by Kind): the create unit of work calls svc.quota.Reserve TWICE in the same *Tx for a service whose kind consumes a per-subtype dimension — first the existing QuotaResourceServices reservation (counts every service), then a conditional `if service.Kind == ServiceKindXxx { Reserve(QuotaResourceXxx) }` AFTER it and BEFORE the services.Insert. Order matters: the services reservation locks first so a tenant who exhausts the services dimension cannot acquire the per-subtype counter at all; the per-subtype reservation locks second so a tenant who exhausts ONLY the per-subtype dimension still gets a typed apierr.QuotaExceeded whose ExceededDetail.Resource names the per-subtype dimension specifically. Both reservations roll back together on any later failure (Insert, Enqueue, audit Append). Test pattern (clone of BE-0325): TWO quota_usage rows + TWO quota_policies seeded (per-subtype TIGHT at 5, services LOOSE at attempts*2 so the per-subtype dimension is the boundary that bites), assert detail.Resource == QuotaResourceXxx, assert BOTH active reservation tables == success count (because every winning Create reserved one of each). A second non-concurrent test exhausts the per-subtype dimension then proves a sibling-kind Create (compose / database for BE-0326) still succeeds — pins the per-subtype boundary as a regression barrier against a future refactor that drops the Kind guard. BE-0326 already lands the kind-guard inside ServiceService.Create at the same line where Reserve(services) returns nil; BE-0327 and BE-0328 each add ONE more conditional Reserve call right below t