ralph/progress.txt:32506:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go — repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants for the quota_usage table. quota_usage is the per-tenant counter table; its ONLY public write surface in QuotaRepository is LockUsage, which INSERTs the counter row ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING and then SELECTs FOR UPDATE. There is no per-row Update/Delete method, no version column, and no service_id pointer — the increment path is owned by quota.Checker and the delete-cascade is owned by schema CASCADE. The 9-test file pins what neither quota_repository_test.go (the original LockUsage happy-path test) nor the schema/tenant-isolation siblings already cover: (1) row-shape on the INSERT leg — qusg_ prefix, used_value defaults to 0, created_at/updated_at within 1s; (2) second-call byte-identity — id, created_at, updated_at, AND used_value all anchored across ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING (the set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only; a non-UPDATE must not stamp updated_at — proved with a measurable wallclock gap between calls); (3) peer-row byte-identity — LockUsage(org, services) does not touch the (org, projects) counter row's id/created_at/updated_at/used_value, and the two rows have distinct ids; (4) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING discards the minted qusg_ id when the row exists — proved by pre-seeding a row at used_value=17 with id `qu_seed_projects` and observing both the live used_value and the original id survive a LockUsage call; (5) FK violation (unknown organization) -> typed Conflict via mapWriteError; (6) DOMAIN violation by casting `"not-a-resource"` to store.QuotaResource -> typed Conflict; (7) transaction rollback — closure returns sentinel after a successful LockUsage, Write rolls back, no quota_usage row persists (counter probe = 0); (8) nil-tx -> yerr.CodeInternal. The 9th test is the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter that the file does NOT duplicate — it lives in quota_repository_test.go and remains the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe.
ralph/progress.txt:32524:  - **Repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants test file for a TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an external code path (quota.Checker), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — single-table INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO, because ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire — quota_policies's UPDATE leg actively REFRESHES updated_at via the trigger, so its byte-identity proof EXCLUDES updated_at; quota_usage's byte-identity proof INCLUDES updated_at as a load-bearing anchor (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof for quota_usage is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (the UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) constraint isolates rows by resource within a tenant), distinct from quota_policies's cross-scope peer (plan_default vs organization within the SAME quota_policies table); (c) the "row already exists with non-zero value" leg has no quota_policies analog — for quota_usage we pre-seed a row at used_value=17 via direct SQL (seedQuotaUsage from usage_test.go) and prove BOTH the seeded id `qu_seed_projects` AND the seeded used_value survive the LockUsage call, which is the only way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted qusg_ id does not replace the row's id (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT (...) DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would replace the row's id and reset used_value to 0 — a catastrophic data-loss path the typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to store.QuotaResource — `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")` — to bypass Go's compile-time check on the typed string and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer; this is the same casting pattern quota_policies uses for unknown enforcement_mode (`store.EnforcementMode("not-a-mode")`).
